From 334764cedbee490d5c8a65c36cdf75d1e84fbf16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 04:13:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] intersect: apply -F and -r to each database record under
 -split

BlockMgr::findBlockedOverlaps tested -F (and the reciprocal test of -r)
against the sum of the block lengths of every database record that touched
the query, with the non-redundant overlap as numerator, and cleared every hit
when that ratio was too small. A record lying entirely inside the query was
therefore dropped as soon as other records also overlapped the query (three
identical reads inside a 100-bp interval: 0 lines under -split -F 0.5, 3 lines
without -split), and a record that failed the fraction on its own was kept
when a neighbour passed. Without -split both options are per record.

Test -F and -r per database record, on that record's own block length, and
keep -f as the cumulative fraction of the query covered by the records that
survive (issue #750). Overlap blocks of rejected records are no longer handed
to coverage.

test/intersect/test-intersect.sh: the -F 0.20 case now also reports exon2 for
two_blocks_two_exons (20 of 100 bases); new cases for three identical reads
(-F 0.5, -F 1.0) and the issue #1142 example (-split -r -f 0.90).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
